### 5. HTTP Keyserver Protocol (HKP)
- **Presentation:** [HKP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-openpgp-hkp-00)
- **Presenter:** Andrew Gallagher
- **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-openpgp-hkp`

Andrew Gallagher highlighted changes and open questions in `draft-ietf-openpgp-hkp`:

- **Recent Changes:** Added `Link: <...>; rel="next"` HTTP headers to support pagination on prefix log queries; updated v2 submission terminology to use "certs"; corrected IANA templates based on feedback from Songbo Peng.
- **Open Architectural Questions:**
  - Evaluation of `.well-known` paths versus legacy hardcoded paths.
  - Grandfathering existing SRV record usage for keyserver discovery and round-robin lookups.
  - **Canonical Bundles & Merge Policies:** Discussed the conflict between strict HTTP REST semantics (where `PUT` followed by `GET` returns exact uploaded bytes) versus keyserver merge policies (e.g., retaining existing revocations). Merge Request 10 proposes server-enforced merge policies, whereas Merge Request 12 (by Daniel Huigens) requires clients to resolve conflicts.
- **Identity Equivalence Sets:** Andrew Gallagher raised the concept of treating bound v4 and v6 certificates as composite objects ("identity equivalence sets"). Daniel Kahn Gillmor cautioned against creating new abstract meta-certificates, emphasizing that key replacement is a transition mechanism and normal single-certificate semantics should remain the end goal. Heiko Schäfer noted that many users will maintain v4 and v6/PQC keys in parallel for an extended transition period.

