### 7. Persistent Symmetric Keys
- **Presentation:** [Persistent Symmetric Keys](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-openpgp-persistent-symmetric-keys-00)
- **Presenter:** Daniel Huigens
- **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-openpgp-persistent-symmetric-keys`

Daniel Huigens presented progress on `draft-ietf-openpgp-persistent-symmetric-keys`:

- **Recent Updates:** Clarified usage scope, added signed/encrypted test vectors, provided key/IV derivation pseudo-code, and added deployment guidance (marking PSK certifications non-exportable and recommending deletion of PK-ESK packets when re-encrypting).
- **Interop Testing & Packet Criticality:**
  - Testing key bundles containing both a PSK and a v6 asymmetric key revealed that several implementations (Sequoia, pgp-less, rmp, GnuPG) fail when a PSK packet appears first because they do not handle unknown non-critical packets correctly per RFC 9580.
  - Andrew Gallagher noted that GnuPG 2.5.24 has introduced preliminary support for v6 and ML-KEM.
  - Heiko Schäfer and Daniel Kahn Gillmor reiterated that RFC 9580 requires compliant implementations to ignore unknown non-critical packets, and bugs should be filed against implementations failing on non-critical packets.
- **WGLC Readiness:** The draft is close to WGLC; final consensus regarding packet criticality will be confirmed on the mailing list.

